<code>assert !this.isEmpty() => this.peek().compareTo(\old(this.peek())) >= 0;</code>

However, since `peek()` returns the head of the queue and the head of the queue should be the smallest element, the correct assertion should check that the new head is greater than or equal to the old head only if the removed element was the head. Given the nature of the priority queue, a more accurate assertion would be to check that the new head is still the smallest element after the removal. Here's a revised version:

<code>assert !this.isEmpty() => \forall int j; 0<=j && j<this.size(); this.peek().compareTo(this.get(j)) <= 0;</code>

This ensures that after removal, the new head is indeed the smallest element in the queue. However, given the original comment, a simpler and more direct assertion would be:

<code>assert !this.isEmpty() && this.peek().compareTo(\old(this.get(0))) >= 0;</code>

This checks that the new head is greater than or equal to the old head only if the removed element was the head. But considering the priority queue properties, the first assertion is more comprehensive. 

